  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nShortwave passage early this morning combined with a reinforcing\nsurface cold front will create light lake-enhanced showers, \nmainly snow, north of I-80 prior to dawn. The lack of deep \nmoisture combined with increasing subsidence and warm surfaces \nensures no accumulations develop.\n\nOtherwise, the rest of today will be dry will a mix of sun and\ndiurnally driven cumulus that fade through the afternoon. Look\nfor cirrus to ramp up during the evening to overnight hours and\nlessen the degree of cooling heading into Wednesday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles remain steady in the overall synoptic pattern for\nThanksgiving into the early weekend, though some minor variances\nin depth/forward speed remain. This projection features today's\nupper troughing over the Rockies to deepen/close near the lower\nOhio and Tennessee River Valleys by Thursday, lifting/deepening\na surface low from Florida to the Mid-Atlantic in the process. \nA secondary lobe of upper energy/troughing will kick this \ninitial wave/surface low through the New England region Friday \ninto Saturday, with some sort of long-wave pattern setting up \nthereafter.\n\nFor Thursday, northward movement of the surface low combined\nwith jet-aided ascent aloft favors widespread rain and\nincreasing easterly wind gusts (through at least midnight, the\nprobability for a change over to snow anywhere in the CWA is\nnear zero). There is potential that some combination of\ndownslope shadowing caused by stronger east winds (more likely\ncause) and/or upper trough dry slotting (less likely to occur\nwith this feature favored to be east of the area) causes dry or\nless expansive rain periods through the day around or just west\nof the Laurel Highlands/WV Higher Terrain. Ensemble means\nhighlight this a bit with accumulations in the 0.5\"-1.25\" range\nin that area versus 0.75\"-1.75\" in an axis across eastern OH to\nfar northwest PA. Regardless, given prior dry conditions and\nlower rates, flooding is not expected. \n\nWind gusts may be the bigger impact in the area as current mean\n24 max wind gusts range from 35-45mph with greater speeds \npossible in the higher terrain as a strong easterly low level \njet develops. Wind Advisory or near High Wind Warning gusts \ncan't be ruled out; reaching these higher ends are dependent on\nthe surface low trending deeper and the inversion height being \njust right to offer the ideal cap to induce greater downslope \nwind generation. Winds will remain elevated Friday after the \nsurface cold front passage shifts winds from the east to the NW \nbefore tapering off with waning surface pressure gradients \nSaturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe latest round of ensemble guidance trended slightly toward\nmaintaining a stronger NW flow type pattern into Saturday in the\nwake of the exiting low; in addition, their more bullish on\nwrapping a bit more moisture into the backside of this system,\nincreasing QPF amounts slightly during the period that the\ncolder air is in place. This has increased the probabilities for\naccumulating snow in the higher terrain (lower elevations may\nsee light snow mix in with light rain in on-off showers during\nthis period). Ending 7pm Saturday, current probabilities suggest\na 40-75% chance of 1\" or greater along the Laurel Highlands/WV \nHigher Terrain, whereas 24 hours prior, those probabilities sat\nbetween 20-40%. Now, the likelihood of seeing Advisory-level\nsnowfall remains low (not to mention likely melting of any snow\nin between showers during the daytime), but it bears monitoring\nin the coming days which way the trend continues.",
